## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to model non-crossing, higher-arity structures with DP and reconstruct a solution.

## Key Concepts

- DP state dp[i][t] for i and t
- Non-crossing triple formation
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Time/space trade-offs and optimization path

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTriples(A, n, k) {
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) {
        for (let i1=1; i1<i; i1++) {
          for (let j=i1+1; j<i; j++) {
            const prev = (i1>=2 ? dp[i1-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : -Infinity));
            const val = prev + A[i1-1]*A[j-1]*A[i-1];
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = {i1, j}; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction logic would backtrack using prv
  return {value: dp[n][k], triples: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a range-based segment cost and path reconstruction, a common advanced pattern not covered yet.

## Key Concepts
- Contiguous partitioning
- Range query (max-min)
- DP with backtracking
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  // base
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let p = i - 1; p >= 0; p--) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[p]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[p]);
      dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i)) - Math.min(...A.slice(0, i));
    }
  }
  // transitions would fill dp[i][t] and cut[i][t]
  return { cost: dp[n][k], partition: cut };
}
```

